          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The consensus identifies theologically significant knowing as intimate, experiential, relational, and often covenantal knowledge rather than mere factual awareness; knowing God includes acknowledgment, fidelity, love, and obedience, with sexual intimacy as a concrete related use.\",\"book_summary\":\"The book identifies knowing as covenant relationship acknowledged and demonstrated through faithful obedience, encompassing marital intimacy and recognition by a covenant lord, not mere mental assent.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Personal, experiential covenant relationship rather than merely intellectual awareness.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Covenant relationship recognized and evidenced by faithful obedience rather than merely intellectual awareness.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"No material boundary disagreement is evident. The book emphasizes obedience as the proof of covenant knowledge, but the consensus already includes obedience and covenant fidelity within the relational meaning. Its movement from marital intimacy to divine acknowledgment also tracks the consensus cases.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. The book neither replaces relational knowledge with another referent nor denies the consensus identification.\",\"rationale\":\"Reduced to their minimum cores, both readings define symbolic or theological knowing as lived covenant relationship rather than cognition alone. The book's emphasis on faithful obedience is an articulation of how such relationship is recognized, already represented in the consensus through Jeremiah 22 and 1 John 2. Its marital and lord-vassal applications likewise correspond to the consensus's sexual-intimacy and covenantal-acknowledgment senses.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"The phrase “recognized and proven by faithful obedience” could be read as narrowing genuine knowledge to cases visibly authenticated by obedience, whereas the consensus sometimes describes relational or experiential knowledge more broadly. However, the cited 1 John text and the consensus itself treat obedience as integral evidence, so this does not create a material difference.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
